WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.601
Hi, I'm Gideon!

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:07.406
Let me show you how Naive-N0.5-Flash runs a million-token context without

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:09.153
ever doing full attention.

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:11.247
Here's the trick.

00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:13.040
Each layer reads only a little.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.731
A short window, or a sparse pick of keys.

00:00:15.731 --> 00:00:16.927
No layer reads everything.

00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:21.388
The context can grow all the way to a million tokens.

00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:23.953
Thirty-nine layers are sliding windows.

00:00:23.953 --> 00:00:27.386
Each one reads only the last 128 keys.

00:00:27.805 --> 00:00:29.301
Nine layers are sparse.

00:00:29.301 --> 00:00:33.789
An indexer keeps the top 2,048 keys, and only those are read.

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:36.491
So no layer ever reads all million.

00:00:36.491 --> 00:00:40.076
Per token the work stays near twenty-three thousand keys, and flat.

00:00:41.667 --> 00:00:44.152
A window layer sees only a few tokens back.

00:00:44.572 --> 00:00:47.926
A sparse layer scores every key, then keeps just the best ones.

00:00:48.346 --> 00:00:51.378
What you never see is a dense layer reading the whole square.

00:00:53.008 --> 00:00:56.635
Keep those nine layers dense instead, and at a million tokens they

00:00:56.635 --> 00:00:58.751
would read about four hundred times more.

00:00:58.751 --> 00:01:00.262
That is the whole saving.

00:01:01.675 --> 00:01:06.077
Their runtime, NaiveRT, decodes it at a reported two thousand one hundred

00:01:06.077 --> 00:01:10.479
twenty-two tokens a second, fusing a whole sparse layer into one kernel.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.121
The lab says AI designed the architecture and built the runtime, while

00:01:16.121 --> 00:01:18.802
humans set direction and made the key calls.

00:01:18.802 --> 00:01:20.812
A claim about process, not proof.

00:01:22.475 --> 00:01:27.164
Drop dense attention entirely, and a frontier model's million-token context becomes a

00:01:27.164 --> 00:01:29.508
fixed cost, not a runaway one.

00:01:31.192 --> 00:01:32.403
No dense layers.

00:01:32.403 --> 00:01:37.245
Thirty-nine windows, nine sparse pickers, and a million-token context that stays cheap.

00:01:37.665 --> 00:01:39.766
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.366
I'm Gideon.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:40.666
Bye!
